WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.400
When I started playing magic, mulligans seemed&nbsp;
like a largely uncomplicated subject. If your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:11.400
hand is okay, you keep it. If it sucks, you&nbsp;
don’t. And for the most part, this analysis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:16.000
reflected the actual state of the game. See,&nbsp;
for the first several years I played the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.320
the official mulligan rule was the Paris&nbsp;
mulligan. If you don’t like your hand of seven,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.920
you can draw a new hand of six. If you don’t&nbsp;
like that hand, you can draw a new hand of five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.280
and so on. In some competitive constructed&nbsp;
applications you might have planned out some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:35.240
sort of mulligan policy for yourself, but the&nbsp;
general idea, especially in limited, was, uhh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:41.520
try not to mulligan, because it sucks, especially&nbsp;
with regard to mana screw. If your deck is 40%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:48.160
lands, a seven card hand averages 2.8 lands.&nbsp;
If you mulligan after drawing too few lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:53.720
the six card replacement hand will average&nbsp;
2.4 lands. God forbid you need to go to five,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.360
because that hand will average two lands, and&nbsp;
will have a greater probability of having one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:04.520
land hand than having three lands. Yikes! In&nbsp;
addition, if there’s a couple of pivotal cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:09.440
in your deck, every mulligan you take means a&nbsp;
turn later that you’ll see one of those cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:15.440
meaning that your midgame potential is compromised&nbsp;
as well. However, this all changed in 2019.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:20.320
The introduction of the London mulligan meant&nbsp;
that, since you get to see a full seven cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:25.280
with every mulligan, it's no longer the painful&nbsp;
choice it used to be. This is all the more true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:30.120
in commander, where you get one for free. The&nbsp;
filtering value in being able to pitch a hand to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:35.440
get that same number of cards again is legitimate,&nbsp;
and shouldn’t be underestimated. Taking that first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:40.160
mulligan has no cost to you other than that it&nbsp;
requires taking your first mulligan, so if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.760
aren’t using that mulligan regularly, every few&nbsp;
games at least, you’re actively giving up on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:50.600
resource the game is handing you. In addition,&nbsp;
going down to six cards, or even to five cards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:56.280
isn’t that big of a cost for a lot of decks. It’s&nbsp;
a bit painful in limited because card effects and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:00.720
power levels are less diverse, so drawing&nbsp;
a different set of lands, generic beaters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:06.480
and noncreature spells often won’t feel worth the&nbsp;
cost of losing one of them. However, in commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:12.480
cards vary wildly. Despite my personal wishes for&nbsp;
the format, just about every deck runs a piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.840
fast mana that will dramatically warp its early&nbsp;
turns if drawn, and there are a number of other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.720
cards that will similarly have an outsized&nbsp;
impact if drawn in the opening hand—other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:28.680
low cost ramp spells like Birds of Paradise, low&nbsp;
cost draw spells like Mystic Remora or Skullclamp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:35.240
and high impact creatures such as Professional&nbsp;
Facebreaker or Academy Manufactor. A six or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:40.760
five card hand with one of the deck’s best cards&nbsp;
will often be a lot better than your average seven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.560
card. In addition, as I mentioned in my lands&nbsp;
video, the existence of a commander creature means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.480
that decks will have a pre-selected card that’s&nbsp;
effectively in-hand at all times. This means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:57.600
when you mulligan, you know that whatever hand you&nbsp;
draw will have access to that creature—as long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:03.320
you like your commander, it’s literally impossible&nbsp;
for a hand to have access to no good cards.

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:08.360
So, mulligans are a great tool, and developing&nbsp;
your skill in something as superficially simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.120
as saying yes or no to a given hand will generate&nbsp;
you tons of advantage through additional free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.640
filtering in the long term. In addition, not only&nbsp;
are there relevant decisions to be made during a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.920
given game, but the influence of mulligans on your&nbsp;
decisionmaking process begins while you’re still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:29.000
building your deck. We can see an indication of&nbsp;
this immediately with the number of lands people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.640
choose to run in their decks—if everybody had to&nbsp;
keep their first hand every time, I suspect that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:39.920
you’d see way more decks gravitating toward land&nbsp;
counts in the low 40s, the range that maximizes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:45.760
the odds of a three land hand. But nah—if we&nbsp;
draw too few lands, the cost of grabbing a new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:51.200
hand is relatively low due to previously mentioned&nbsp;
factors, and the odds of drawing a workable hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.920
within the first three drawn is pretty high. There&nbsp;
are also people who adhere to this principle much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.680
more strongly than just absentmindedly&nbsp;
tossing 35 lands in all their decks.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.640
There’s a particular person in my comments&nbsp;
section who advocates a land count of around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:10.360
28 for most lower-curved decks, with the&nbsp;
logic being that an aggressive mulligan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:15.320
policy can be employed to guarantee a high&nbsp;
probability of drawing into enough lands to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:20.800
function. And let’s consider that plan for a&nbsp;
moment—a given hand in a deck with 28 lands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:26.960
will have a 36% chance of having 0 or 1&nbsp;
lands, a 33% chance of having two lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:33.280
and a 31% chance of having three or more lands. If&nbsp;
we’re willing to mulligan to 6, to see up to three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:38.600
hands of cards, this gives us a 95% chance&nbsp;
of seeing a hand with at least two lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:43.440
and a two thirds chance of seeing a hand with at&nbsp;
least three lands. These aren’t the worst odds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:48.280
especially when you consider that there might be&nbsp;
low cost draw, low cost tutors, low cost ramp,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:55.320
landcycling, and other tools that can make a&nbsp;
two or even one land hand keepable. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.840
this also calls back to the idea of mulligans as&nbsp;
a resource—getting to see more hands of cards has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:06.680
filtering value, and constructing a deck in&nbsp;
the 28 land framework means that filtering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:12.640
resource is basically spent exclusively looking&nbsp;
for the right land count. Now, this can be fine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.160
if you really trust your nonland cards to be&nbsp;
valuable and trust your manabase to give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:22.720
the symbols you need, but you’re still giving up&nbsp;
a bit of ability to hunt for good nonland plays.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:27.840
As a demonstration of this, we can think of a&nbsp;
hand that has two lands and some desirable nonland&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:33.480
cards. Decent hand, I’d happily keep it. But what&nbsp;
if we’d just made some changes to the deck, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:39.000
one of these lands got cut for a Ledger Shredder.&nbsp;
Suddenly, this hand has a very high risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.720
missing at least one early land drop, and we might&nbsp;
need to mulligan for a different hand, a hand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:49.440
will likely have worse nonland plays. The point&nbsp;
here is that running fewer lands will lead to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.000
greater frequency of needing to reject hands with&nbsp;
desirable nonland plays. If we’re looking for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:01.160
hand with 2-4 lands, running a land count of 30&nbsp;
will give us that 66% of the time, whereas a land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:07.640
count of 40 will give us that 76% of the time.&nbsp;
10% might sound like a small difference, but think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:12.440
about it this way—almost a third of the hands that&nbsp;
the hypothetical 30 land deck needs to mulligan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:17.760
due to land count are keepable by a 40 land deck,&nbsp;
and those hands might contain some really great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:23.920
cards. This doesn’t mean that running more lands&nbsp;
is objectively correct—there definitely are decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:29.600
where having a lower chance to topdeck a land is&nbsp;
especially desirable—but it does demonstrate some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:34.800
of the tradeoffs involved with running different&nbsp;
land counts, and the way that higher land counts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:40.200
and better manabases for that matter, can&nbsp;
increase the filtering value of the mulligan step.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:44.920
Let’s say you decide to lean more in the direction&nbsp;
of filtering for nonland cards. What’s a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.920 --> 00:06:49.320
thing to do with this filtering? Well, a lot of&nbsp;
decks will have a particular thing that they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:54.680
looking to find—for example, a piece of ramp is a&nbsp;
frequent want for decks with a very specific curve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.680 --> 00:07:00.960
in mind, as is the case with the Goreclaw deck I&nbsp;
mentioned in my lands video. In cases like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.240
how many of a particular type of card&nbsp;
a deck should run often comes down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:10.360
whether the deck is okay with drawing multiple&nbsp;
copies. In my lands video I showed a formula&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:14.920
for finding the probability of drawing&nbsp;
a card of a type, and while 20 cards of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:20.520
a type does give a comfortable 80% chance&nbsp;
of drawing at least one in any given hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:25.600
the flip side is that there’s also a more&nbsp;
than 40% chance of drawing two or more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:32.200
and there actually a lower chance to draw exactly&nbsp;
one copy than there is with a count of 15,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:37.600
or even 10. Now, some decks won’t mind multiple&nbsp;
copies of whatever they’re looking for, but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:42.880
a deck looking for a diverse range of cards,&nbsp;
or a deck that is tight on space, running 10-12&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:48.600
copies and mulligan-ing aggressively for them&nbsp;
might be a better plan than running 15 or 20.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:53.240
Another consideration that’s especially relevant&nbsp;
for higher powers is that decks can be built with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:58.960
the intent to mulligan in different ways based on&nbsp;
the opposing decks and the turn-order. In certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:03.120
matchups it might be correct to mull aggressively&nbsp;
for a hand that will make a bunch of bold plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:08.320
early in a rush to gain dominance, whereas a&nbsp;
different deck pool and turn order might make a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:13.880
grindier hand with quality draw and interaction&nbsp;
a much more appealing one to stick with.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:18.440
At this point, I think I should mention—I’ve been&nbsp;
presenting mulligans as a filtering resource,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:22.520
which might imply that each deck has a set&nbsp;
quantity of this resource that it should spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:28.000
all of. And this is the easiest way to think of&nbsp;
it, but it’s not exactly correct. Willingness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:33.400
to mulligan will differ person-to-person and&nbsp;
deck-to-deck, and how tightly a given player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:39.520
leverages their mulligans will generally depend&nbsp;
on three factors. One of these factors is how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:44.360
they care about the quantity of cards they start&nbsp;
the game with. I mentioned that constructed EDH&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:51.160
decks have more card variation than limited decks,&nbsp;
and this effect scales with power level. In cEDH,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:57.000
a single power card like Dockside Extortionist or&nbsp;
Esper Sentinel might redeem an otherwise-painful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:02.880
five card hand, and a high density of specific&nbsp;
stax pieces means that ditching a hate card&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.640
against three commanders that aren’t generally&nbsp;
that affected by it isn’t a large cost at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:13.160
Another factor that will influence mulligan&nbsp;
willingness is a player’s risk tolerance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:17.800
and this actually works in both directions.&nbsp;
A player who wants to sit down at the table&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:22.320
and have a game where they’re guaranteed to do&nbsp;
something might be willing to keep a slow hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.440
rather than risking finding a worse one, and a&nbsp;
different player could use the same reasoning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:31.560
to mulligan aggressively for a great hand because&nbsp;
they don’t trust their topdecks. On the flip side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.520
we can imagine daredevils of multiple stripes&nbsp;
boldly keeping or mulligan-ing and believing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:41.480
their deck to deliver them greatness. Ultimately,&nbsp;
this factor is a matter of personal preference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:46.040
more than anything, and will mainly come&nbsp;
down to what different people find fun.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:50.720
The final factor in determining mulligan&nbsp;
willingness is how confident a player feels in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.960
their mulligan-ing skill. Less experienced players&nbsp;
and players who don’t know their deck as well will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.960 --> 00:10:01.320
often be inclined to keep a mediocre-but-workable&nbsp;
hand that a more experienced player would happily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:06.400
toss out to hunt for a better one. This is a skill&nbsp;
you can work on, and the best way to do this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.440
to understand the ratios of different things in&nbsp;
your deck and how likely each is to get drawn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:16.200
either in an opening hand or in the first several&nbsp;
draws of the game. It’s useful to know that your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:21.560
opening hands will contain an average of 2.5&nbsp;
lands and 2.2 cards above three mana, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:28.040
they’ll have a 70% chance each to contain at least&nbsp;
one piece of ramp and draw. If this feels like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:33.240
nebulous process to put into practice, you can&nbsp;
also do lots of playtesting—draw opening hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:37.760
play out the first several turns, rinse and&nbsp;
repeat. And actually, doing a combination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:43.600
these two—pre-play analysis and playtesting—is&nbsp;
the quickest way to build intuition as to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:49.040
various deckbuilding choices will turn into&nbsp;
practical early-game results. It’s easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:54.800
see your library as a mystical block box, to&nbsp;
deify it as an entity that will either bless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.800 --> 00:11:01.440
you with wonderful cards or curse you with all the&nbsp;
wrong stuff. But it’s not that—the way the cards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:06.360
in your library turn into hands is predictable&nbsp;
in a probabilistic sense, and getting a better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:14.000
feel for that will improve your ability to play&nbsp;
your deck, both in the mulligan phase and beyond.

